    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-66509",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/be5b7625ad6f540f6769b121f52ccad69b669dbf764fc3bb68f89c99f066f145.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?",
      "worked_solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/bfc7d94de67ceeb0c4aaa0988c117fce7f15242c2f3d223eecd8f0eb77135d7e.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graph IV, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV shows a one-to-one function. This means that  the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II and  III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
